  "subject": "⏰ Axios Finish Line: Outsmart your clock",
  "date": "Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:29:38 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🛍️ Plus: Double kindness | Wednesday, November 18, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 18, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 695 words ... 2½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: How to fake being an early bird \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Whether we're early birds or night owls is mostly baked into our genetics . And it generally makes sense to live our lives by our own internal clocks.\n\n But if you've entered a new life phase — say, you've become a parent, started a job with an earlier schedule or decided to make time for morning workouts — you may need to learn to rise and shine. Why it matters: The world is built for early birds. For many of us, mornings offer the best chance to carve out time for ourselves before the demands of the day take over.\n\n The good news: Research shows there are steps we can take to fake being morning people, even if that's not how we're wired. 🔬 The science: In a 2019 Sleep Medicine study , researchers helped 22 night owls shift their internal clocks roughly two hours earlier in just three weeks.\n\n The changes: Earlier bedtimes, more morning sunlight, less evening light, consistent mealtimes and avoiding caffeine and exercise late in the day. Participants reported less stress and depression and performed better on morning cognitive and physical tests. Another study, published in Nature Communications in 2022, found that how awake and alert we feel in the mornings is influenced by factors we can control, including sleep, exercise and breakfast.\n\n 🛌 Case in point: Corey Buhay , a Colorado-based adventure journalist, writes for Outside magazine that she struggled so much to wake up early that her mother would pull away her pillows and sheets, then push her onto the floor. Buhay would keep sleeping — even there.\n\n Now, after a year of experimenting with her routine, Buhay wakes up by 6 a.m. most days, and exercises four or five times a week. How she did it: Buhay shared the tricks that helped her become an early riser. Among them:\n\n 😎 Make mornings fun: Plan an activity you look forward to, like a walk followed by a drink from your favorite coffee shop. Buhay writes that she'd plan fun workouts for the morning because \"if it's a workout I'm likely to skip at 11 a.m., I definitely will at 6.\" 👭 Use your friends: Make early plans with friends so someone's counting on you to wake up and show up. Buhay quips: \"If I only have to meet myself at the gym, I'll stand me up every time.\" 🏋️‍♀️ Lower the bar: Buhay would pledge to do just one set of squats or 15 minutes of stretching at the gym. You can always do more. 🚿 Get a head start: If you've naturally got energy at night, use it! Pack your workout bag, prep breakfast or shower before bed to make mornings easier. Buhay even sleeps in her running clothes. ☕ Don't forget weekends: Sleeping in on Saturdays and Sundays can throw off your internal clock. Give yourself a reason to get up and moving on weekend mornings, too. The bottom line: You might never love mornings. But you can learn to make them work for you.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Break through the noise with Axios \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Reach execs and business leaders with Axios.\n\n\r\n We'll help you tell your story in the right way:\n\n\r\n \r\n We'll distill your brand's message into its most effective form with Smart Brevity. \r\n No clutter, no filler — just clean, smart and effective. \r\n \r\n Contact us to learn more. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🛒 Decades-old kindness \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n This evening's random act of kindness — times two — happened more than 50 years ago. But it has stayed with the recipient, Peggy Elmhorst of New Orleans, ever since:\n\n \"I was on a very tight budget, in the grocery, picking and choosing what would see me through for two weeks, when the next paycheck would appear.\" \"As I was checking out, I realized I had somehow lost my cash. I asked the clerk if I could go look for it on the floor, but the manager had it. Some kind soul had found it and turned it in. I was feeling so thankful that I would be able to pay for my purchases, when I was told by the clerk that the person originally behind me in line had paid for my food.\" \"Two kindnesses! I was 23 years old. Today, I'm 80 and have never forgotten how much kindness can change situations for people. I have tried to be worthy by passing it on.\" 📮 Share the bits of goodness you've witnessed in the world — along with your name and hometown — with finishline@axios.com . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
