nt-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: How to fake being an early bird </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/Bgos9iwAAUMICqHrp6weyZWyGhs=/1920x1080/smart/2024/11/04/212910-1730755750147.jpg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of a happy bird with a worm next to a disheveled owl.\"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Whether we're early birds</strong> or night owls is mostly <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjIvMDMvMjkvbmlnaHQtb3dsLWVhcmx5LWJpcmQtc2xlZXAtaW50ZXJuYWwtY2xvY2s_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBf0378c22\" target=\"_blank\">baked into our genetics</a>. And it generally makes sense to live our lives by our own internal clocks.</p><ul><li><strong>But if you've entered</strong> a new life phase — say, you've become a parent, started a job with an earlier schedule or decided to make time for morning workouts — you may need to learn to rise and shine.</li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The world is built for early birds. For many of us, mornings offer the best chance to carve out time for ourselves before the demands of the day take over.</p><ul><li><strong>The good news: </strong>Research shows there are steps we can take to fake being morning people, even if that's not how we're wired.</li></ul><p><strong>🔬 The science: </strong>In a <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly9wdWJtZWQubmNiaS5ubG0ubmloLmdvdi8zMTIwMjY4Ni8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB1fd9eca5\" target=\"_blank\">2019 Sleep Medicine study</a>, researchers helped 22 night owls shift their internal clocks roughly two hours earlier in just three weeks.</p><ul><li><strong>The changes: </strong>Earlier bedtimes, more morning sunlight, less evening light, consistent mealtimes and avoiding caffeine and exercise late in the day.</li><li><strong>Participants reported</strong> less stress and depression and performed better on morning cognitive and physical tests.</li></ul><p><strong>Another study, </strong>published in <em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubmF0dXJlLmNvbS9hcnRpY2xlcy9zNDE0NjctMDIyLTM0NTAzLTI_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBdcedc993\" target=\"_blank\">Nature Communications</a></em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cubmF0dXJlLmNvbS9hcnRpY2xlcy9zNDE0NjctMDIyLTM0NTAzLTI_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aCdcedc993\" target=\"_blank\"></a> in 2022, found that how awake and alert we feel in the mornings is influenced by factors we can control, including sleep, exercise and breakfast.</p><p><strong>🛌 Case in point: </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuY29yZXlidWhheS5jb20vYWJvdXQ_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB6a75b108\" target=\"_blank\">Corey Buhay</a>, a Colorado-based adventure journalist, writes for <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cub3V0c2lkZW9ubGluZS5jb20vaGVhbHRoL3dlbGxuZXNzL2hvdy10by1iZWNvbWUtbW9ybmluZy1wZXJzb24vP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBe1c62823\" target=\"_blank\">Outside magazine</a> that she struggled so much to wake up early that her mother would pull away her pillows and sheets, then push her onto the floor. Buhay would keep sleeping — even there.</p><ul><li><strong>Now, after a year </strong>of experimenting with her routine, Buhay wakes up by 6 a.m. most days, and exercises four or five times a week.</li></ul><p><strong>How she did it: </strong>Buhay <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cub3V0c2lkZW9ubGluZS5jb20vaGVhbHRoL3dlbGxuZXNzL2hvdy10by1iZWNvbWUtbW9ybmluZy1wZXJzb24vP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aCe1c62823\" target=\"_blank\">shared the tricks</a> that helped her become an early riser. Among them:</p><ol><li><strong>😎 Make mornings fun: </strong>Plan an activity you look forward to, like a walk followed by a drink from your favorite coffee shop. Buhay writes that she'd plan fun workouts for the morning because \"if it's a workout I'm likely to skip at 11 a.m., I definitely will at 6.\"</li><li>👭 <strong>Use your friends:</strong> Make early plans with friends so someone's counting on you to wake up and show up. Buhay quips: \"If I only have to meet myself at the gym, I'll stand me up every time.\"</li><li>🏋️‍♀️ <strong>Lower the bar: </strong>Buhay would pledge to do just one set of squats or 15 minutes of stretching at the gym. You can always do more.</li><li>🚿 <strong>Get a head start:</strong> If you've naturally got energy at night, use it! Pack your workout bag, prep breakfast or shower before bed to make mornings easier. Buhay even sleeps in her running clothes.</li><li><strong>☕ Don't forget weekends: </strong>Sleeping in on Saturdays and Sundays can throw off your internal clock. Give yourself a reason to get up and moving on weekend mornings, too.</li></ol><p><strong>The bottom line: </strong>You might never love mornings. But you can learn to make them work for you.</p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtNzIxYjhlMjAtMDc5OS00M2EwLTgwMTktNzc1OGYyOGZhNjVmLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBebef2d3f\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Tweet this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-linkedin-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Post to LinkedIn\"></a>  <a href=\"mailto:?subject=From Axios: 1 big thing: How to fake being an early bird&body=teruyuki%40hh.iij4u.or.jp%20has%20shared%20an%20Axios%20story%20with%20you%3A%0A%0A1 big thing: How to fake being an early bird%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2Fnewsletters%2Faxios-finish-line-721b8e20-0799-43a0-8019-7758f28fa65f.html%3Fchunk%3D1%26utm_term%3Demshare%23story1\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-email-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Email this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-sms-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Text this Story\"></a></td></tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      \r\n<tr><td height=\"20\" style=\"height: 10px; font-size: 0;\">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td class=\"ol-adpad\" style=\"padding: 20px 3.5% 0 3.5%;\"><p class=\"b\"\r\nstyle=\"-webkit-text-size-adjust: 100% !important; margin-bottom: 0 !important; font-size: 11px !important; letter-spacing: 1.4px; color: #008dc8; font-weight:700;\"\r\n>\r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n</p></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td style=\"padding: 20px 3.5%; line-height: 1.5;\"><span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size: 26px; color: #222222;  font-family: 'gorditamedium', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;  letter-spacing: -0.1px; line-height: 1.5;\">Break through the noise with Axios</span></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td height=\"10\" style=\"height: 10px; font-size: 0;\">&nbsp;</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td>\r\n<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48499577.262234/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL2FkdmVydGlzZT91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1hZCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249ZmluaXNoX2xpbmVfYWR2ZXJ0aXNpbmcmdXRtX2NvbnRlbnQ9aG91c2VfYWRfbWlkJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB174e649a\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"ht